#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * githooks/commit-msg — TRACKED canonical source for `.git/hooks/commit-msg`
 * (FEAT-045, the enforcing half of the commit-identity control demoted from
 * claude-author-guard.js). Installed via claude-committhook-install.js
 * (`node claude-committhook-install.js install`). `.git/hooks/` itself is
 * never version-controlled (AC-11) — this file is what survives a fresh
 * clone; the installed copy does not.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHY commit-msg, NOT pre-commit AND NOT prepare-commit-msg (AC-1).
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Live evidence gathered against real git 2.55 on this machine, in throwaway
 * repos, before this file was written:
 *
 *   `git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT` and `git var GIT_COMMITTER_IDENT`, run from
 *   inside `pre-commit`, `prepare-commit-msg`, `commit-msg`, and
 *   `post-commit` hooks, ALL FOUR correctly reflected a `--author` override
 *   and a distinct GIT_COMMITTER_NAME/GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL override on this
 *   git version. Identity resolution ALONE does not distinguish the three
 *   pre-commit-family hooks here — this is a materially different result
 *   from received git-hook lore that `pre-commit` cannot see `--author`;
 *   that lore may be version-dependent and is not what this repo's git does.
 *
 *   What DOES distinguish them is VERB COVERAGE: a real `git merge --no-ff`
 *   merge commit fires `commit-msg` (confirmed) but does NOT fire
 *   `pre-commit` at all (re-confirmed cleanly, see the ASM-386 note below —
 *   an intermediate draft of this file briefly mis-stated this point after
 *   a flawed re-test, then corrected it back after re-testing properly; see
 *   claude-committhook.test.js's "AC-1 evidence" tests for the clean,
 *   out-of-repo-marker/always-fail-hook reproduction this claim now rests
 *   on). A guard installed only at `pre-commit` would therefore never see a
 *   merge commit's identity on this machine.
 *
 *   `commit-msg` is chosen because it is the LATEST point that still runs
 *   BEFORE the commit object is written (so a rejection here truly prevents
 *   the commit — nothing to undo, no working-tree/index cleanup needed) AND
 *   covers `commit` + `merge`, which `pre-commit` alone does not.
 *
 * ==== ADDITIONAL FINDING, made during the build of this file (ASM-386, P0) ====
 *
 *   Independent of the pre-commit-vs-commit-msg question above: on this
 *   same git version, NEITHER `pre-commit` NOR `commit-msg` fires AT ALL for
 *   `git cherry-pick`, `git revert`, or `git am` — verified three
 *   independent ways (Node spawnSync + real commit-count fixtures, an
 *   always-exit-1 hook + commit counting, and the identical always-exit-1
 *   hook run directly in Git Bash with no Node layer involved). `commit`
 *   and `merge` ARE protected by this hook; `cherry-pick`, `revert`, and
 *   `am` currently are NOT, on this environment. This was NOT part of the
 *   acceptance file's own verified evidence (it logged this specific gap as
 *   an unverified P1 assumption, not a confirmed finding) — logged here as
 *   ASM-386 (P0) now that it is confirmed. Root cause not determined (a
 *   Git-for-Windows-specific dispatch difference for sequencer-based
 *   commands is suspected, not confirmed) and left for Bill/Aaron; NOT
 *   silently worked around here. `commit-msg` remains the right choice of
 *   the two hook points tested for the verbs it DOES cover — it is simply
 *   not, by itself, a complete answer to all five KNOWN_COMMIT_VERBS on
 *   this machine.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * IDENTITY SOURCE (AC-2): git's OWN resolution, never parsed text.
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This hook determines the commit's author and committer by invoking
 * `git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT` and `git var GIT_COMMITTER_IDENT` (BOTH —
 * BUG-035's original finding was that author and committer are separate
 * fields and checking only one is a hole) and extracting the email from
 * each RESOLVED identity string. It never reads GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL /
 * GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL env vars directly — those are one INPUT to the
 * resolution, not the resolution itself, and are absent when the override
 * came from `--author` or `-c` rather than an env var. There is also no
 * shell command visible to a commit-msg hook to parse in the first place —
 * it receives only a path to the commit-message file as argv[2] ($1 in a
 * shell hook), which this hook does not even need to read (this item's
 * scope is identity only, not message/content inspection — see
 * tool.destructiveguard, FEAT-040, for code-bearing detection).
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * THE GAPS THIS DOES NOT CLOSE — TWO OF THEM, NOT ONE.
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * (1) AC-15, out of scope, stated not solved: `git commit --no-verify`
 * bypasses commit-msg ENTIRELY — this is git's own native, always-available
 * escape hatch, present for every hook on every git repository. Closing it
 * is not possible from a hook, by construction: the hook simply is not
 * invoked. No paired CI authorship check exists either — explicitly ruled
 * out by Aaron ("local-only scope is accepted").
 *
 * (2) ASM-386, P0, NOT out of scope, NOT deliberately accepted — a live gap
 * discovered building this file: `git cherry-pick`, `git revert`, and
 * `git am` do not invoke this hook AT ALL on this machine's git (see the
 * CORRECTION note above). Unlike (1), this was not a scope decision anyone
 * made — it means this hook's real guarantee, TODAY, on THIS environment,
 * is "a commit made via `git commit` or `git merge` cannot fabricate an
 * identity," not "...via any of the five KNOWN_COMMIT_VERBS." Flagged to
 * Bill/Aaron for a follow-up decision (investigate the root cause; find a
 * hook point that DOES cover the sequencer verbs; or accept the narrower
 * guarantee explicitly) — this file does not attempt to paper over it.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FAIL-CLOSED — the OPPOSITE posture of the demoted claude-author-guard.js.
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Unlike the demoted PreToolUse guard (which now fails OPEN on every
 * internal error — see that file's header), THIS hook is FAIL-CLOSED: a
 * `git var` invocation failure, the shared sanctioned-identity module
 * (claude-author-identity.js) throwing, or any other internal error DENIES
 * the commit (non-zero exit, no commit object written). The two layers
 * deliberately disagree because they have different costs of being wrong:
 * this hook is the ONLY remaining hard stop for a normal `git commit`, so it
 * fails closed; the demoted guard is no longer a hard stop at all, so it
 * fails open. Do not "fix" this file to match the guard's posture — that
 * would silently reopen the exact hole this item exists to close.
 *
 * Verb scope: KNOWN_COMMIT_VERBS is not consulted here — there is no verb
 * dispatch inside this hook to get wrong; every invocation of this hook is,
 * by construction, one of the verbs that DOES reach it. The point that
 * needs restating (see ASM-386 above) is that on this environment, that set
 * is `commit` and `merge` only, NOT all five KNOWN_COMMIT_VERBS — this
 * hook simply is never invoked at all for `cherry-pick`/`revert`/`am`
 * here, so there is nothing for this hook's own logic to get right or
 * wrong for those three; the gap is upstream of this file, in git's own
 * hook dispatch on this machine.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FEAT-046: a SECOND check, added to this SAME hook slot — GR#22 codename
 * content scan, coexisting with the identity check above.
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * `commit-msg` is a single named hook — git invokes at most one script
 * there. FEAT-045 (identity checking, above) already occupies this slot, and
 * FEAT-046 (Codename Discipline, GR#22 — the reference title must never
 * enter git as file content) needs the same hook point for the same reason:
 * merge-commit coverage that `pre-commit` structurally cannot provide (see
 * claude-codename-content-scan.js's own header for that item's independent
 * live confirmation of the same AC-1 finding, for the content-scan case
 * specifically). Rather than installing a second, competing `commit-msg`
 * script (which git cannot run — only one script occupies the slot — so
 * doing that would SILENTLY DROP one of the two controls), this file calls
 * BOTH checks in sequence: identity first (above), then the codename content
 * scan (below). Either check denying the commit denies the commit.
 *
 * The codename content scan itself — pattern set, fragment-assembly
 * technique, and boundary logic — lives ENTIRELY in claude-codename-
 * patterns.js and claude-codename-content-scan.js (GR#3: this file defines
 * no pattern of its own, and is not the PreToolUse `claude-codename-
 * guard.js`'s replacement — that advisory layer is retained unchanged,
 * firing earlier and also covering command text and branch names that this
 * git-level hook does not see).
 *
 * FAIL-CLOSED (same posture as the identity check above, OPPOSITE of the
 * demoted claude-author-guard.js): a `git diff --cached` failure or the
 * shared pattern module throwing denies the commit — see
 * claude-codename-content-scan.js's header for the full reasoning (a
 * codename leak into a public repo cannot be withdrawn, unlike an identity
 * mismatch which costs a human seconds to correct).
 *
 * DISCLOSED LIMITATIONS, not solved here (see claude-codename-content-
 * scan.js's header for the full text):
 *   (1) AC-10 — `cherry-pick`/`revert`/`am` are structurally uncovered, same
 *       as the identity check's own ASM-386 gap above.
 *   (2) AC-11 — an interactively-composed commit MESSAGE BODY (not passed
 *       via `-m`) is scanned by NEITHER this hook (which inspects the
 *       staged diff, not `$1`) NOR the PreToolUse guard (which has no
 *       command-string content to scan for an editor-composed message).
 *       Flagged as an Escalation in the acceptance file
 *       (docs/planning/acceptance/tool.codenamehook.md) for Bill/Aaron,
 *       not resolved unilaterally here.
 */

'use strict';

const { execFileSync } = require('child_process');
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');

/** Resolves claude-author-identity.js relative to THIS file's own location,
 * which differs depending on how this file is currently being run:
 *   - INSTALLED (production): <repo-root>/.git/hooks/commit-msg, so
 *     __dirname is <repo-root>/.git/hooks — TWO levels up reaches
 *     <repo-root> (found the hard way: one level up only reaches
 *     <repo-root>/.git, which does not have it — a real bug this item's own
 *     AC-1/AC-3 throwaway-repo end-to-end tests caught immediately).
 *   - TRACKED SOURCE, required directly (tests): githooks/commit-msg, so
 *     __dirname is <repo-root>/githooks — only ONE level up reaches
 *     <repo-root> (claude-author-identity.test.js requires this file this
 *     way, to assert the two consumers share the exact same function
 *     references — see AC-4).
 * Existence-checked rather than guessed, so wherever this file legitimately
 * lives, it finds the real module — no path-shape assumption baked in that
 * only holds for one of the two cases. */
function resolveIdentityModulePath() {
  const candidates = [
    path.join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'claude-author-identity.js'), // installed
    path.join(__dirname, '..', 'claude-author-identity.js'), // tracked source, required directly
  ];
  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) return candidate;
  }
  throw new Error(
    `claude-author-identity.js not found relative to ${__dirname} ` +
      `(tried: ${candidates.join(', ')})`
  );
}

/** Same two-candidate resolution shape as resolveIdentityModulePath() above
 * (FEAT-046), for the codename content-scan module — this hook's installed
 * location differs from its tracked-source location the same way, so the
 * same existence-checked, no-path-shape-assumption approach applies
 * unchanged. */
function resolveCodenameScanModulePath() {
  const candidates = [
    path.join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'claude-codename-content-scan.js'), // installed
    path.join(__dirname, '..', 'claude-codename-content-scan.js'), // tracked source, required directly
  ];
  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) return candidate;
  }
  throw new Error(
    `claude-codename-content-scan.js not found relative to ${__dirname} ` +
      `(tried: ${candidates.join(', ')})`
  );
}

const identity = require(resolveIdentityModulePath());
const codenameScan = require(resolveCodenameScanModulePath());

/** Denies the commit: non-zero exit, message to stderr. commit-msg running
 * before the commit object is written means a non-zero exit here writes
 * NOTHING — the index and working tree are untouched (AC-3/AC-16 — proven
 * by real subprocess tests, not by inspecting this branch). */
function fail(reason) {
  process.stderr.write(
    `\n🛑 COMMIT-MSG IDENTITY HOOK (FEAT-045) — commit REJECTED:\n\n${reason}\n\n` +
      `This check cannot be bypassed except by git's own native ` +
      `\`git commit --no-verify\` escape hatch — see this file's header, ` +
      `AC-15 (out of scope by construction, not by choice).\n`
  );
  process.exitCode = 1;
}

/** Same shape as fail() above, but for the FEAT-046 codename content-scan
 * check — a distinct message so a denied operator immediately knows WHICH
 * of the two checks in this hook slot fired, rather than a generic "commit
 * rejected" that leaves them guessing between an identity problem and a
 * GR#22 content problem. */
function failCodename(reason) {
  process.stderr.write(
    `\n🛑 COMMIT-MSG CODENAME CONTENT SCAN (FEAT-046, GR#22) — commit REJECTED:\n\n${reason}\n\n` +
      `The reference title is 'Blue', and only 'Blue'. Its real name, its ` +
      `abbreviations and its numbered form must never enter git as staged ` +
      `content. Rewrite to say 'Blue' or "the reference title" and re-stage.\n\n` +
      `This check cannot be bypassed except by git's own native ` +
      `\`git commit --no-verify\` escape hatch — see this file's header, ` +
      `AC-10/AC-11 (disclosed, uncovered gaps — not this escape hatch).\n`
  );
  process.exitCode = 1;
}

function gitVar(name) {
  return execFileSync('git', ['var', name], { encoding: 'utf8' }).trim();
}

/** Extracts the email from a resolved "Name <email> timestamp tz" identity
 * string, lowercased. Returns null if no "<...>" segment is present. */
function extractEmail(identLine) {
  const m = /<([^<>]*)>/.exec(identLine || '');
  return m ? m[1].trim().toLowerCase() : null;
}

/** Core check, factored out for direct unit-testing (AC-1/AC-2/AC-16
 * tests call this without spawning a subprocess). Returns
 * { ok: true } or { ok: false, problems: [...] } — never throws for a
 * NORMAL unsanctioned identity (that is a problem to report, not an
 * error); DOES throw if `sanctioned` derivation itself is broken, which the
 * caller (main()) is responsible for turning into a fail-closed exit. */
function checkAgainstSanctioned(sanctioned, authorIdent, committerIdent) {
  if (!sanctioned || sanctioned.size === 0) {
    return {
      ok: false,
      problems: [
        'could not derive ANY sanctioned identity (no git config ' +
          'user.email, no qualifying history, no ' +
          'CLAUDE_AUTHOR_GUARD_EXTRA_IDENTITIES).',
      ],
    };
  }
  const authorEmail = extractEmail(authorIdent);
  const committerEmail = extractEmail(committerIdent);
  const problems = [];
  if (!authorEmail || !sanctioned.has(authorEmail)) {
    problems.push(`author identity "${authorIdent}" is not sanctioned.`);
  }
  if (!committerEmail || !sanctioned.has(committerEmail)) {
    problems.push(`committer identity "${committerIdent}" is not sanctioned.`);
  }
  return { ok: problems.length === 0, problems };
}

/** Full entry point, used both by the CLI invocation below and directly by
 * tests. Fail-closed: any throw from git var or from
 * identity.deriveSanctioned() is caught HERE (not left to propagate to a
 * generic top-level handler) so the fail-closed reasoning is explicit and
 * co-located with the rest of this hook's decision logic. */
function main() {
  let authorIdent;
  let committerIdent;
  try {
    authorIdent = gitVar('GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT');
    committerIdent = gitVar('GIT_COMMITTER_IDENT');
  } catch (err) {
    fail(`could not resolve git identity via \`git var\`: ${err.message}\n\nFailing closed.`);
    return;
  }

  let sanctioned;
  try {
    sanctioned = identity.deriveSanctioned();
  } catch (err) {
    // AC-4's "lazy implementation" trap: there is NO fallback list here.
    // A broken shared module means this hook cannot verify anything, so it
    // denies — it does not silently substitute a second, undeclared source
    // of truth.
    fail(`sanctioned-identity derivation failed internally: ${err.message}\n\nFailing closed.`);
    return;
  }

  const result = checkAgainstSanctioned(sanctioned, authorIdent, committerIdent);
  if (!result.ok) {
    fail(
      result.problems.map((p) => `  - ${p}`).join('\n') +
        `\n\nThis machine derives ${sanctioned.size} sanctioned ` +
        `identit${sanctioned.size === 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'} (addresses withheld ` +
        `deliberately — this repo is public). Check your own ` +
        `\`git config user.email\`.`
    );
    return;
  }

  // FEAT-046, AC-8: the second check occupying this same hook slot. Runs
  // only once the identity check above has already passed — either check
  // failing denies the commit; this hook does not need both checks to run
  // to reach a correct final decision, but running the codename scan only
  // after identity passes keeps the two checks' failure messages from ever
  // being conflated (a denied commit always names exactly one reason).
  let hits;
  try {
    hits = codenameScan.scanStagedDiff();
  } catch (err) {
    // AC-7: fail-closed on the codename scan's own internal error — a
    // `git diff --cached` failure or the shared pattern module throwing.
    // Same "no fallback, no silent substitute" reasoning as the identity
    // check's own AC-4 trap above, applied to GR#22 content instead of
    // identity.
    failCodename(`could not scan the staged diff: ${err.message}\n\nFailing closed.`);
    return;
  }
  if (hits.length) {
    failCodename(hits.map((h) => `  - ${h}`).join('\n'));
    return;
  }

  process.exitCode = 0;
}

if (require.main === module) {
  try {
    main();
  } catch (err) {
    // Defense in depth: main() already catches its own known failure modes
    // and fails closed explicitly; this is the fail-closed backstop for
    // anything that still escapes (e.g. an unreadable commit message file
    // path in a future revision that reads argv[2] — not currently read by
    // this hook, but AC-16 names it as a class this hook must fail closed
    // on if it is ever added).
    fail(`internal error: ${err.message}\n\nFailing closed.`);
  }
} else {
  module.exports = {
    gitVar,
    extractEmail,
    checkAgainstSanctioned,
    main,
    deriveSanctioned: identity.deriveSanctioned,
    // FEAT-046: exported so tests can exercise the codename-scan wiring
    // directly (e.g. monkeypatching scanStagedDiff to throw for AC-7,
    // asserting the same require target as claude-codename-guard.js for
    // AC-4/AC-5) without needing a fresh subprocess for every case.
    scanStagedDiff: codenameScan.scanStagedDiff,
  };
}
